ibility so to  conclude from this study and to kind of  make a take-home message point is that  young individuals who have insulin  resistance may have worsened cognitive  performance even if it's with them  age-related norm in the absence of the  weight gain whereas being obese in the  older age is associated with both  cognitive performance even in the  absence of this metabolic dysfunction of  insulin resistance moving along to  disorders of effective regulation or  depressive illnesses so their number of  studies done in our lab and many other  labs over the years over the last 15  years which accumulate a certain number  of experiments and contribute to the  evidence that patients with depression  may in fact have profound contribution  of the insulin resistance to their  illness one of the more intriguing and  and kind of difficult things to accept  is that the age at which insulin  resistance may affect mood disorders  actually also moving unfortunately to  the younger age and the study which I  will not show yet because it's still  ongoing suggests that even in  adolescence with depression we can see  already neural correlates of insulin  resistance but for now it would suffice  to say that insulin resistance is often  accompanied by depressive symptomatology  and that patients with depression health  biomarkers associated with insulin  resistance so the next question would be  naturally okay so if there is this an  overlapping pathophysiological platform  what would happen if we treat the  patient with depression for the insulin  resistance so what would happen it would  try to resolve in fluent assistance and  depressed individual and I will show you  here the study  which were published a few years ago as  well on the air Don of the PRG agonist  which is the insulin sensitizing agent  in this case it was pioglitazone versus  placebo in patients who had in fact  being treated for depression and  remained depressed  despite being treated for it what we  found was simple basically if if you add  PRG agonist to the treatment as usual  for depression over three months period  there is an appreciable decline in the  depression severity and so a lot of  patients respond to that medication so  therefore there is a improvement in  depressive symptomatology  as a result of reduction in insulin  resistance what is even more interesting  that there is an innate effect back to  the just previously mentioned study on  the age-related cognitive performance  the age effect in this in this study was  that the younger individuals respond  better so there is a more enhanced  improvement in depression severity in a  younger group than in those who were a  little bit older  now I will move move that goalpost  a little bit further and talk about the  biomarkers of stress and the allostatic  load as they may underlie the responses  and be a mechanistic goalpost for the  prediction of the response to the  resolving insulin resistance in  disorders of brain telomeres have been  on a de for post of the research for the  last fifteen to twenty years they were  discovered by Elizabeth Blackburn here  at UCSF which she received a Nobel Prize  we all know that telomeres are basically  the caps of the regular chromosomes they  are known to be associated with  accelerated aging and could be a  biomarkers of multiple processes  associated with premature aging of  course one of the processes we would  talk about today would be inflammation  and oxidative stress and insulin  resistance and so then their  multiplicity of various studies  suggesting that telomeres are viable and  fairly easy obtainable predictor for the  effects of the cardiovascular disease or  diabetes or dementia in the brain  especially in older individuals but  again looking at the younger individuals  in trying to provide a an individualized  approach targeting the these processes  early in life so we looked at the  telomeres and I will move again the  slide without necessarily g